<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0

  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the macOS build — the peer of
  electron/build/entitlements.mac.plist, and DELIBERATELY SHORTER than it.

  Apple's hardened runtime is required for notarization, and it switches off
  several things an app may need. Each entry below is present because something
  concrete breaks without it; each entry the Electron peer has and this one does
  NOT is absent because this shell genuinely does not do that thing.

    disable-library-validation
      Valve's `libsteam_api.dylib` is not signed by Apple and is not signed with
      OUR team id either. Library validation refuses it, which surfaces as the
      game launching with Steam silently unavailable rather than as a crash.
      (The Electron peer needs this for a second reason as well — steamworks.js'
      native `.node` addon — which has no counterpart here: the Steam binding is
      compiled INTO this binary and carries our own signature.)

    allow-dyld-environment-variables
      Steam launches games with DYLD_* set. Without this the app is killed on
      launch when started FROM Steam, while working perfectly when
      double-clicked — a confusing failure to debug.

    device.audio-input
      VOICE CHAT records the player's microphone (stamped per build —
      shell/src/capabilities.rs). The hardened runtime refuses the device
      without this entitlement, and refuses it SILENTLY: `getUserMedia` rejects
      with a permission error indistinguishable from the player having said no.
      It pairs with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in the bundle's Info.plist, and
      like that key it is present in every macOS build — an entitlement nothing
      asks to use costs a build without voice nothing, while a missing half of
      the pair is a feature that fails on one platform only.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why:

    allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      The Electron peer needs both because V8 lives inside the app and compiles
      JavaScript to machine code in the app's own address space. WKWebView does
      not: it runs the page in its own out-of-process content and JIT services,
      which carry Apple's entitlements rather than ours. Granting an app
      permission to write executable memory it never writes is a hole with no
      feature behind it, so this shell does not ask for it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
